WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.090
All right, well, we're no longer
right in the old city of Kraków.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:09.360
We took the tram, which is over there,
and we came across the Vistula River

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:13.890
and we're looking at, uh, Kazimierz,
if I have it correctly, which is

00:00:13.895 --> 00:00:18.660
where the Jewish ghetto is located,
and we're gonna be walking, we're

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:20.460
taking a walking tour this afternoon.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.870
And what's interesting about this is,
you know, you form an impression of

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:27.870
a city because for the first three
days we stayed in the old city.

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:32.310
We thought this is Kraków, uh,
inside the walls of the old city.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:35.700
And then we begin to realize that
Kraków is actually much larger.

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:39.510
And of course it's much larger because
the central part of Kraków within the

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:41.129
city walls is actually quite small.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:44.350
So here's another part, Kazimierz.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:48.544
And I, I think there's more and more to
this city, and again, I bring everything

00:00:48.544 --> 00:00:49.955
back to language learning, right?

00:00:50.584 --> 00:00:53.675
But so I think of it, you know, when
you're learning a language, you've got

00:00:53.735 --> 00:00:57.635
the big picture, the whole language,
which you gradually get bits and pieces

00:00:57.635 --> 00:01:02.164
of and where whatever part of it you
have, you think that's the language.

00:01:02.164 --> 00:01:04.745
But of course the language
is always much larger.

00:01:05.315 --> 00:01:11.805
And, you know, visiting Kraków
here, I bought a book, which is

00:01:12.595 --> 00:01:17.055
Historia Krakowa dla kazdego.

00:01:17.345 --> 00:01:20.275
So it describes the history of Kraków.

00:01:20.295 --> 00:01:22.545
It explains the different
parts of Kraków in Polish.

00:01:22.545 --> 00:01:26.715
So this is good for my overall Polish
language, getting the language in me.

00:01:26.715 --> 00:01:30.315
I understand 60, 70,
80, 90% of what I read.

00:01:30.395 --> 00:01:32.414
I don't worry about
what I don't understand.

00:01:32.625 --> 00:01:34.455
I'm still getting the language in me.

00:01:34.455 --> 00:01:35.985
I'm getting an impression of Kraków.

00:01:36.465 --> 00:01:42.975
I also bought a book on learning polish
for Ukrainians, and I'm going through this

00:01:43.244 --> 00:01:47.625
and I'm picking up the bits and pieces
of grammar that I have looked at before.

00:01:47.955 --> 00:01:52.335
And every time you look at the
grammar, it's just a, an ingredient.

00:01:52.664 --> 00:01:55.484
And I kind of see the whole
process of language learning is...

00:01:55.875 --> 00:02:00.945
it's almost like you're, you're mixing a
dough and the dough gets bigger and bigger

00:02:00.945 --> 00:02:03.015
as you throw more ingredients into it.

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:06.825
The, the ingredients are the words and
the phrases that you pick up as you're

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:10.115
reading and you're listening and maybe
you're talking to people and sometimes

00:02:10.914 --> 00:02:14.100
I, when I talk to people in Polish,
I can't quite manage, and we go back

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.330
to English, but it doesn't matter.

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:19.200
They're all bits and pieces that
are fed into this big dough.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:24.300
And then the visit with the bits
and pieces, the visit to the

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:28.860
grammar, where I don't necessarily
remember it, but it is contributing.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:36.600
It's contributing to solidifying my grasp
on this overall picture of the language.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:40.740
And the same way here, we're gonna
travel to different parts of Kraków.

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:44.190
We're gonna talk to people, take the
tram, which is a great experience.

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:47.160
I love taking a local
transit when I'm traveling.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:50.440
And then we're gonna have a walking
tour, which will be in English.

00:02:51.494 --> 00:02:55.545
But this evening we might speak
some Polish, and every so often I

00:02:55.785 --> 00:03:00.015
consult my grammar book, which has
the advantage of being in Ukrainian.

00:03:00.015 --> 00:03:04.005
So I'm looking at the differences
between Polish and Ukrainian.

00:03:04.755 --> 00:03:09.015
And then tomorrow we're taking the train
back to Warsaw and we have a meetup.

00:03:09.274 --> 00:03:14.894
And, um, there we are gonna,
I'm gonna speak Polish, uh,

00:03:30.239 --> 00:03:30.739
How do you say?

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:33.299
And, uh,

00:04:02.385 --> 00:04:06.825
So the important thing is to, in
my conversation, I will make lots

00:04:06.825 --> 00:04:10.185
of mistakes, but it's another
engagement with the language.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.030
I will hear what people are saying.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:15.239
If I have to think about whether
this is correct, is it a Ukrainian

00:04:15.239 --> 00:04:17.849
word or a Russian word or a
Czech word or a Polish word?

00:04:18.089 --> 00:04:18.839
Is it correct?

00:04:18.839 --> 00:04:19.920
Is it the correct tense?

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:23.159
I won't be able to speak, but if
I can start speaking and listening

00:04:23.164 --> 00:04:24.900
to people, it's further input.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:29.580
It's further, further involvement with
the language and gradually slowly, as I

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:34.020
said the other day, the language, if you
get enough opportunity, you will improve.

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:38.580
And I have enough of a hold on
Polish now I can read this book and

00:04:38.585 --> 00:04:42.615
understand most of it that if I were
to spend two months in Poland, I

00:04:42.615 --> 00:04:43.935
would be speaking quite comfortably.

00:04:44.205 --> 00:04:49.125
But of course here with, uh, five days
exposure, I can't speak very comfortably,

00:04:49.365 --> 00:04:54.885
but you'll have a chance to hear me
at our meetup on Thursday afternoon.

00:04:55.305 --> 00:04:58.935
June the first in front of
the Puro Hotel in Warsaw.

00:04:59.345 --> 00:05:00.945
Bye For now.

00:05:01.125 --> 00:05:05.015
So here we are in the square, in Warsaw.

00:05:06.565 --> 00:05:13.000
Uh, we just went for a walk to get over
our, having spent so much time eating on

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:21.480
a plane and uh, in behind us we have, we
walked a fair distance to get here and

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:23.340
you can see the whole thing behind us.

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:26.730
I can show you there what it looks like.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:28.320
The old town.

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:29.560
The old town.

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:33.480
So now we are going to
proceed into the old town.

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:37.830
I spent two months listening and
reading, working up my vocabulary.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:44.190
I even worked on the plane on my
iPad with my history of Poland and

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:48.170
I've got my vocabulary up to where I
even decided to buy some paper books.

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:53.160
One is, uh, Help Yourself book
and the other is a history or the

00:05:53.165 --> 00:05:55.170
Life of Chopin, all in Polish.

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:59.730
I'm gonna read them, it's gonna help me,
but trying to speak has been difficult.

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:02.010
I struggled with my taxi driver.

00:06:02.015 --> 00:06:04.020
I struggled on the plane
with the stewardess.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.410
And didn't quite get there, but we'll see.

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:09.090
Couple of days I should improve.

